{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The claimed quantitative law for cross edges is false. In the final fixed point the bichromatic edges are exactly the edges between the two initial 2-cores. Since each core has expected size Θ(n^{1/3}), the expected number of edges between two such subsets of a 3-regular graph is at most O(|A-core||B-core|/n) = O(n^{1/3} n^{1/3}/n) = O(n^{-1/3}), not Θ(n^{1/3}). The target's own matching characterization only gives an upper bound of Θ(n^{1/3}); it does not show that a constant fraction of core vertices are cross-matched. Thus the prevalence law for cross edges is off by a factor of n^{2/3}.","target":"3d82a99f15462e0ee4b2c100a21b9130c83c68a78d2909bd2de0fbfea28ecede"}]}